// The build book, written in Cufet.
//
// ★★ MOSTLY A GATE. This book introduces no type and offers exactly one member, `checksum`, which
// exists to decide staleness and is FOR BLUEPRINTS ONLY — pulling this book is a file's
// declaration that it is a build description, so reaching for the member from an ordinary program
// is off-label. ⚠ That is why the walker riding along with the book is not a coupling bug: a
// program pulling the book inherits the builder, so a compiled one needs a way to launch a child.
// ✅ It has one on every platform now — the terminal form builds on Windows through `_spawnvp`
// since 2026-09-13, so a blueprint COMPILES rather than only running interpreted. A blueprint's
// steps are STRUCTURAL RECORDS, so nothing has to be handed across the book boundary at all —
// `Pull a book on blueprints.` exists so a file can DECLARE WHAT IT IS, and so `cufet build` can
// refuse a build description that does not say so, rather than treating a bare function name as
// magic. `chance` and the language books are the same shape: a name, and nothing else.
//
// ★ Why a record and not a `step` type: BookLoading.MakePrivate renames everything a book layer
// declares beside its module to `<name> in <book>`, so a book CANNOT hand out a nominal type
// without native machinery — which is why `matrix` and `chase` are C# types rather than Cufet
// ones. `regex.cufe` crosses the same boundary the same way. A structural record costs nothing
// and needs no registration.
//
// A step is `a record with (the text name, the series of text needs, the series of text makes,
// the series of text runs)`:
//   name   — what to call it when reporting
//   needs  — paths that must exist before it runs, and which order it against other steps
//   makes  — paths it produces, which is how another step comes to need it
//   runs   — argv, program first
//
// ★★ THE GRAPH IS NEVER WRITTEN DOWN. A step that needs a path another step makes must run
// second, and that is the whole of the ordering rule — there are no edges to declare. Staleness is
// by content hash, so `needs` has to be declared regardless; declaring edges as well would state
// one fact twice, and the two could then disagree with nothing to catch it.
//
// ⚠⚠ EVERY NAME THIS FILE BINDS IS PREFIXED `bp-`, and that is not decoration. This file is
// spliced into EVERY Cufet program, and a top-level function's local shares a scope with a
// program's shared constants — an unprefixed `Define total` here would make `Define total as 42
// permanently.` refuse to compile in every program on earth. See the same warning in `regex.cufe`,
// which is where that was measured.
//
// ⚠ The walker is reached by a driver statement `cufet build` APPENDS to the parsed program, the
// way the parser reaches `match in regex`. It is deliberately not a module member: a blueprint's
// author never calls it, and a member would invite them to.

// No module declaration, and that is load-bearing: a book WITH a Cufet layer binds at the
// layer and its BookType disappears (CheckPullStatement), which would put the native `hash`
// member out of reach. `blueprints` has no Cufet members, so it needs no layer.

/// Is this path produced by a step that has not run yet?
///
/// ★ This is the whole of the ordering rule. A step waits exactly as long as something it needs is
/// still owed by a step that has not run — no edges, no graph structure, just the two lists.
Bind fact to bp-owed, given (
    the series of records like (the text name, the series of text needs, the series of text makes, the series of text runs) bp-plan,
    the series of fact bp-ran,
    the text bp-path):
    Define bp-at as 0.
    For each bp-step in bp-plan, repeat:
        Increment bp-at by 1.
        If item bp-at of bp-ran is false:
            For each bp-made in the makes of bp-step, repeat:
                If bp-made is bp-path, return true.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
    Return false.
Done.

/// What a file's contents amount to, as one word — or "missing" when there is no such file.
///
/// ⚠⚠ THE SELF-PULL IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT A MISTAKE. `checksum` is a member of this book, and
/// a plain function reaches a module only where it is WRITTEN — a module's methods inherit what
/// their caller pulled, and a free function has no caller to inherit from. So the book pulls
/// itself. The checker knows this pull came from the prelude and does NOT record it as the
/// PROGRAM's pull, which is what keeps an unpulled `blueprints` out of every compiled binary.
/// See `_inPrelude` in TypeChecker.Core, and BookDroppingTests, which pin both directions.
///
/// ★ "missing" rather than a failure, and rather than an empty word. A file that does not exist has
/// an answer — and because the answer goes into a signature, an input APPEARING has to read as a
/// change. It does: "missing" is not any checksum.
Bind text to bp-stamp, given (the text bp-path):
    Pull a book on blueprints.
        Define bp-sum as blueprints's checksum of (bp-path).
        If bp-sum is void, return "missing".
        Return "{bp-sum}".
    Done.
Done.

/// One line standing for everything a step depends on: its argv, and the contents of every input.
///
/// ★★ THE ARGV IS PART OF IT, and that is not padding. A step whose inputs are untouched but whose
/// flags changed produces a different result, so a signature over inputs alone would skip exactly
/// the rebuild the change was made to cause.
///
/// ⚠ The two lists are separated by a marked field, so an argv word can never be read as a path.
/// Without the markers, moving a word from one list to the other would change what the step means
/// and leave the line identical.
Bind text to bp-signature, given (
    the text bp-name, the series of text bp-runs, the series of text bp-needs):
    Define bp-line as "{bp-name}|runs".
    For each bp-word in bp-runs, repeat:
        The bp-line becomes "{bp-line}|{bp-word}".
    Done.
    The bp-line becomes "{bp-line}|needs".
    For each bp-need in bp-needs, repeat:
        The bp-line becomes "{bp-line}|{bp-need}|{cast bp-stamp on (bp-need)}".
    Done.
    Return bp-line.
Done.

/// Is everything this step claims to produce actually there?
///
/// ★ `checksum` answering void for a file it cannot read IS the existence test — there is nothing
/// separate to ask. A deleted output shows up here rather than in the signature, which is why the
/// two questions are asked separately and a step is skipped only when both agree.
Bind fact to bp-made-already, given (the series of text bp-makes):
    For each bp-made in bp-makes, repeat:
        If cast bp-stamp on (bp-made) is "missing", return false.
    Done.
    Return true.
Done.

/// What the last build recorded, a line per step.
///
/// ★ A failure is an empty answer, not an error. Having no record is the ordinary state of a
/// project nobody has built yet, and the first build must not be the one that reports a problem.
Bind series of text to bp-remembered:
    Define bp-lines as a series of text.
    Try to:
        The bp-lines becomes read all lines from the file ".cufet-build".
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        Define bp-none as a series of text.
        The bp-lines becomes bp-none.
    Done.
    Return bp-lines.
Done.

/// The line the last build recorded for this step, or "" if it recorded none.
///
/// ⚠⚠ THE WHOLE LINE, never a reconstruction of it. An earlier version returned just the signature,
/// by splitting on the separator and rejoining everything after the name — and it NEVER MATCHED: a
/// signature's last field can be empty, so the split yields a trailing blank and the rejoin puts
/// the separator back. Every step rebuilt every time. ★★ It cost nothing visible, which is the
/// whole lesson — a build that rebuilds too often looks exactly like one that works. So only the
/// first field is ever read, and what comes back is the text that went in.
Bind text to bp-recalled, given (the series of text bp-lines, the text bp-name):
    For each bp-line in bp-lines, repeat:
        Define bp-fields as bp-line split by "|".
        If the number of bp-fields is greater than 0:
            If item 1 of bp-fields is bp-name, return bp-line.
        Done.
    Done.
    Return "".
Done.

/// Everything recorded except this step's line — what the new one is added to.
Bind series of text to bp-forgotten, given (the series of text bp-lines, the text bp-name):
    Define bp-kept as a series of text.
    For each bp-line in bp-lines, repeat:
        Define bp-fields as bp-line split by "|".
        Define bp-mine as false.
        If the number of bp-fields is greater than 0:
            If item 1 of bp-fields is bp-name, the bp-mine becomes true.
        Done.
        If bp-mine is false, insert bp-line into bp-kept.
    Done.
    Return bp-kept.
Done.

/// Put down what has been built, all of it, as it stands.
///
/// ★ Called after EVERY step that runs rather than once at the end, so a build that fails halfway
/// keeps what it did and the next one carries on instead of starting over.
///
/// ⚠ A record that cannot be written is a MESSAGE, not a failure. Losing the record costs one
/// rebuild; refusing to build because the record could not be kept costs the build.
Bind void to bp-remember, given (the series of text bp-lines):
    Define bp-written as "".
    For each bp-line in bp-lines, repeat:
        The bp-written becomes "{bp-written}{bp-line}\n".
    Done.
    Try to:
        Write bp-written to the file ".cufet-build".
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        State "cufet build: could not record what was built: {the message of the failure}".
    Done.
Done.

/// Run every step, each one after whatever it needs.
///
/// ⚠ A WORKLIST, never recursion — the same lesson `regex.cufe` learned the hard way, where an
/// epsilon closure written as recursion ran the WASM stack out on a pattern both native backends
/// handled. Depth belongs in the heap.
///
/// ★ Repeatedly taking the first ready step is quadratic in the number of steps. That is the right
/// trade here: a build graph is tens of steps, not millions, and the simple rule is the one a
/// reader can check against the sentence above it.
Bind void to bp-walk, given (
    the series of records like (the text name, the series of text needs, the series of text makes, the series of text runs) bp-plan):
    Define bp-ran as a series of fact.
    For each bp-step in bp-plan, repeat:
        Insert false into bp-ran.
    Done.

    // ⚠⚠ ONCE, before the loop, and never again. Re-reading per step would let a build see its own
    // writes: a step that just recorded its line would be read back as current by a later step
    // asking about the same name, and the record would start agreeing with itself. The signatures
    // are recomputed live from the files anyway — this is only what the LAST build left behind.
    Define bp-cache as cast bp-remembered.

    Define bp-left as the number of bp-plan.
    While bp-left is greater than 0, repeat:
        Define bp-pick as 0.
        Define bp-at as 0.
        For each bp-step in bp-plan, repeat:
            Increment bp-at by 1.
            If bp-pick is 0:
                If item bp-at of bp-ran is false:
                    Define bp-ready as true.
                    For each bp-need in the needs of bp-step, repeat:
                        If cast bp-owed on (bp-plan, bp-ran, bp-need), the bp-ready becomes false.
                    Done.
                    If bp-ready is true, the bp-pick becomes bp-at.
                Done.
            Done.
        Done.

        // ★ Nothing is ready and something is left, so every remaining step is waiting on another
        // one. That is a cycle, and it is reported rather than hung on.
        If bp-pick is 0:
            State "cufet build: these steps need each other and cannot be ordered.".
            Exit with 1.
        Done.

        Define bp-doing as item bp-pick of bp-plan.
        Define bp-argv as the runs of bp-doing.

        // ★★ TWO questions, and a step is skipped only when both agree. The signature says the
        // INPUTS and the argv are what they were; `bp-made-already` says the OUTPUTS are still
        // there. Either alone is a wrong answer — an untouched input with a deleted output has to
        // rebuild, and an intact output with a changed input has to rebuild too.
        //
        // ⚠ The signature is taken BEFORE the step runs, which is the only moment it means
        // anything. A step earlier in the walk may have just rewritten one of these inputs, and
        // that is exactly the change this has to see.
        Define bp-line as cast bp-signature on (
            the name of bp-doing, bp-argv, the needs of bp-doing).
        Define bp-was as cast bp-recalled on (bp-cache, the name of bp-doing).
        Define bp-current as bp-line is bp-was.
        If bp-current is true:
            If cast bp-made-already on (the makes of bp-doing) is false, the bp-current becomes false.
        Done.

        // ★ A SKIPPED STEP SAYS NOTHING. The output of a build with nothing to do is empty, and
        // that emptiness is the report. ⚠ It also means a wrongly-skipped step is silent, which is
        // why the three ways of going stale are tested and not only the skip.
        If bp-current is false:
            State "cufet build: {the name of bp-doing}".

            If the number of bp-argv is greater than 0:
                Define bp-program as item 1 of bp-argv.
                Define bp-rest as a series of text.
                Define bp-arg-at as 0.
                For each bp-word in bp-argv, repeat:
                    Increment bp-arg-at by 1.
                    If bp-arg-at is greater than 1, insert bp-word into bp-rest.
                Done.

                Try to:
                    Define bp-outcome as run bp-program with the terminal with arguments bp-rest.
                    If the exit-code of bp-outcome is not 0:
                        State "cufet build: {the name of bp-doing} failed with {the exit-code of bp-outcome converted to text}.".
                        Exit with 1.
                    Done.
                Done.
                In case of failure:
                    State "cufet build: {the name of bp-doing}: {the message of the failure}".
                    Exit with 1.
                Done.
            Done.

            // ⚠ AFTER the step, and only when it succeeded — both `Exit with 1` above leave without
            // reaching here, so a failed step records nothing and rebuilds next time. The whole
            // record goes down each time, so a build that dies later keeps everything up to here.
            The bp-cache becomes cast bp-forgotten on (bp-cache, the name of bp-doing).
            Insert bp-line into bp-cache.
            Cast bp-remember on (bp-cache).
        Done.

        The item bp-pick of bp-ran becomes true.
        The bp-left becomes bp-left - 1.
    Done.
Done.
